{"qid":"Q307697","names":["Zayyanid dynasty"],"title":"Zayyanid dynasty","endonym":{"native":"الزيانيون","lang":"ar"},"capsule":"The Zayyanids (Arabic: الزيانيون, al-Zayyaniyyūn) were a Berber Zenata dynasty that ruled the Kingdom of Tlemcen in northwestern Algeria from 1236 to 1556, founding the city of Tlemcen as a prosperous commercial and cultural center linking the Saharan trade routes to the Mediterranean. The dynasty was established by Yaghmurasen ibn Zyan (r. 1236–1282) and reached its apogee as a regional power in the 13th and 14th centuries, though it was repeatedly pressured by the Marinid sultanate of Morocco to the west and the Hafsid sultanate of Tunis to the east, twice suffering occupation by Marinid forces. The Zayyanid realm finally collapsed under the expanding Ottoman presence in the Maghreb: Tlemcen was taken by the Ottoman corsair Aruj Barbarossa in 1516, and Ottoman suzerainty was permanently imposed by Khayr al-Din Barbarossa in 1556.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territory around Tlemcen and the Tafna basin is well established; the outer extent of Zayyanid control fluctuated markedly under Marinid pressure and is not mapped with precision in surviving sources.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Zayyanid dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zayyanid_dynasty"},{"label":"Britannica: Tlemcen","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Tlemcen"}],"britannica":"topic/Abd-al-Wadid-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Zayyanid dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Yaghmurasen ibn Zyan","nativeName":"يغمراسن بن زيان","roman":"Yaghmurāsan ibn Zayyān","start":123